          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Russell+universes"} Russell universes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/element"} element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A:U
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+universe"} type universe
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            is literally a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \; \mathrm{type}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A:U
            are not literally types, but rather the universe type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            comes with the additional structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+family"} type family
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependent type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(A)
            represents the actual type. Usually universes are defined in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivations"} derivations
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inference+rules"} inference rules
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formation+rule"} formation rule
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+type+family"} universal type family
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi A
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi T
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} type
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \frac{\Gamma \vdash A:U}{\Gamma \vdash T(A) \; \mathrm{type}}
          %p for Tarski universes. Furthermore, universes are usually defined as being closed under all type formers inside the type theory, regardless of the type theory.
          %p
            However, this approach of defining Tarski universes means that the notion of “Tarski universe” differs from type theory to type theory. By this approach, a Tarski universe in the bare intensional
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin-L%C3%B6f+type+theory"} Martin-Löf type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+types"} dependent sum types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/booleans+type"} booleans type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} natural numbers type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pushout+types"} pushout types
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                torus type
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/torus+type"} ?
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncations"} propositional truncations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/W-types"} W-types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localizations"} localizations
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+type"} unit type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+type"} empty type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+numbers+type"} natural numbers type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/booleans+type"} booleans type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+types"} dependent sum types
          %p
            All three notions of Tarski universe described above are in fact definable in all three versions of intensional type theory mentioned above, but Tarski universes are usually described as having, apart from addiitonal internal Tarski universes, exactly the internal type formers that the external type theory has. One usually doesn’t consider Tarski universes with an internal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+construction+type"} James construction type
            in bare intensional Martin-Löf type theory, even though a Tarski universe closed under James constructions is definable in Martin-Löf type theory. On the other hand, in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            the name “Tarski universe” isn’t usually used for a universe only closed under
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+product+types"} dependent product types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+sum+types"} dependent sum types
          %p
            From a global perspective, however, all of these types should be considered Tarski universes, since they model some notion of type of small types, even though the small types in the universe do not behave as the external types in the type theory do. And that is how we approach Tarski universes in this article: Tarski universes are a very general notion encompassing all of the above notions of Tarski universe and more, and one could describe more specific versions of Tarski universes by adding additional structure and axioms to Tarski universes, in the same way one adds additional structure and axioms to an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+group"} abelian group
            to get a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commutative+ring"} commutative ring
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
